Since Theorem \ref{t:char-hke} follows readily from Theorem \ref{t:main} together with known results, we only outline the proof of Theorem \ref{t:main}. The Whitney blending argument proceeds in three main steps:
\begin{enumerate}
\item "We establish a cutoff Sobolev-type inequality" when "the cutoff function is chosen as the equilibrium potential for $K \subset \Omega$, for functions that vanish either in a neighborhood of $K$ or in a neighborhood of $\Omega^c$" (Lemma \ref{l:zerobdy-cs}).
\item Given $f\in\mathcal{F}$, $x_0\in X$, $r>0$, construct $f_1,f_2\in\mathcal{F}$ with $|f|\le |f_1|+|f_2|$, where " $f_1$ vanishes in a neighborhood of $\overline{B(x_0,r)}$" and " $f_2$ vanishes in a neighborhood of $B(x_0,2r)^c$". Apply the previous cutoff Sobolev-type inequality to $f_1,f_2$ to get "a cutoff Sobolev inequality for the equilibrium potential associated with $\overline{B(x_0,r)}\subset B(x_0,2r)$". The key difficulty is "controlling the energies of $f_1$ and $f_2$ in terms of the energy of $f$", handled using "Whitney covers" (Lemma \ref{l:wb}); the construction also uses "the explicit description of the domain of the Dirichlet form" (Proposition \ref{p:domain}).
\item The previous step yields "a non-local analogue of the simplified cutoff Sobolev inequality" (Proposition \ref{p:css}). Then "a self-improvement argument, following \cite[Proof of Lemma~6.2]{Mur24}, then yields the desired cutoff Sobolev inequality" (\textsection \ref{ss:self-improve}).
\end{enumerate}

